// Visual Arts lessons — loaded on demand by ensureLessons('arts') in index.html.
// Shape: LESSON_DB.arts.m<moduleIndex>[topicIndex] = lesson. Order matches COURSE_DATA.arts.
// Artworks are described, never reproduced; look them up in gallery collections to view them.
LESSON_DB.arts = LESSON_DB.arts || {};

// ─── Module 0: Elements & Principles ────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.arts.m0 = {
  0: {emoji:"✍️", subtitle:"Grade 10 · The building blocks every artwork starts with",
    illusText:"A line becomes a shape; add tone and it becomes a form.",
    hook:{title:"Graffiti on the station wall", body:"Look at a graffiti piece on a Johannesburg train-station wall. Thick outlines make the letters pop, the shapes overlap, and shading makes them look 3D, as if they're jumping off the wall. The artist is using the same <strong>elements of art</strong> that Old Masters used — line, shape and form — just with spray cans."},
    intro:"The <strong>elements of art</strong> are the basic building blocks: <strong>line</strong>, <strong>shape</strong>, <strong>form</strong>, <strong>colour</strong>, <strong>texture</strong>, <strong>tone/value</strong> and <strong>space</strong>. <strong>Line</strong> is a mark with length and direction: contour, implied, expressive, gestural; line quality (thick, thin, broken) creates mood — horizontal lines feel calm, diagonal lines feel energetic. <strong>Shape</strong> is a flat, 2D enclosed area: <strong>geometric</strong> (circles, squares) or <strong>organic</strong> (natural, irregular). <strong>Form</strong> is 3D, with length, width and depth (sphere, cube) — or the illusion of it, created with <strong>tone</strong> (light and shadow). <strong>Positive space</strong> is the subject; <strong>negative space</strong> is the area around it.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"〰️ Line", body:"Contour lines show edges; gestural lines show movement; implied lines guide the eye."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⬛ Shape vs form", body:"Shape is flat (2D); form has volume (3D) or looks 3D through shading."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔲 Negative space", body:"The empty space around and between subjects is part of the design."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Mood of line", body:"What feeling do many diagonal lines create?", answer:"<strong>Energy, movement or tension</strong>."},
      {title:"Shape type", body:"The outline of a leaf.", answer:"An <strong>organic</strong> shape."},
      {title:"Shape or form?", body:"A circle shaded with a highlight and a cast shadow.", answer:"It becomes a <strong>form</strong> (the illusion of a sphere)."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Using “shape” and “form” as the same thing.","Ignoring negative space in compositions.","Outlining everything with the same thick line."],
    callout:{title:"Look closely", body:"Blombos Cave on the Southern Cape coast holds engraved and drawn ochre pieces more than 70 000 years old — some of the earliest known marks made by people."},
    activity:"Fill a page with ten different line types (contour, gestural, broken, hatched…). Then draw one object using only contour line, and again using only tone.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. The contour drawing shows edges only; the tonal drawing uses light and dark to show form without outlines.",
    examTip:"In visual analysis, name the element and describe its effect: “Bold diagonal lines create a sense of movement.”",
    keyterms:[{w:"Contour line",d:"A line that follows the edges of a form"},{w:"Organic shape",d:"An irregular, natural shape"},{w:"Negative space",d:"The space around and between subjects"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A flat, enclosed 2D area is a:",opts:["Form","Shape","Texture","Value"],a:1,et:"Forms are 3D."},
      {q:"Diagonal lines usually suggest:",opts:["Calm","Movement","Silence","Sleep"],a:1,et:"Energy and tension."},
      {q:"The space around a subject is:",opts:["Positive space","Negative space","Form","Tone"],a:1,et:"Part of the composition."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Line, shape and form are your alphabet — use them on purpose."},

  1: {emoji:"🎨", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Mixing, matching and creating mood with colour",
    illusText:"Primary → secondary → tertiary. Opposites on the wheel are complementary.",
    hook:{title:"The Ndebele wall", body:"Ndebele house paintings use bold reds, yellows, blues and greens, outlined in black — colours that seem to vibrate next to each other. Esther Mahlangu took these colours and patterns from homestead walls to the world, even painting a BMW art car in 1991. Understanding <strong>colour theory</strong> explains why these combinations are so powerful."},
    intro:"The <strong>colour wheel</strong>: <strong>primary</strong> colours (red, yellow, blue) can't be mixed from others; <strong>secondary</strong> colours mix two primaries (orange, green, violet); <strong>tertiary</strong> colours mix a primary and a neighbouring secondary (red-orange, blue-green). <strong>Colour schemes</strong>: <strong>complementary</strong> (opposites — red/green, blue/orange, yellow/violet — maximum contrast), <strong>analogous</strong> (neighbours — harmonious), <strong>monochromatic</strong> (one hue with tints and shades), <strong>warm</strong> (reds, oranges, yellows — advance, energetic) and <strong>cool</strong> (blues, greens, violets — recede, calm). Colour properties: <strong>hue</strong> (the colour), <strong>value</strong> (light/dark), <strong>intensity/saturation</strong> (bright/dull). A <strong>tint</strong> adds white, a <strong>shade</strong> adds black, a <strong>tone</strong> adds grey.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔴🟡🔵 Primaries", body:"Red, yellow and blue — the starting point for mixing paint."},
      {t:"teal", title:"↔️ Complementary", body:"Opposites intensify each other side by side; mixed, they make browns and greys."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🌡️ Warm vs cool", body:"Warm colours feel close and lively; cool colours feel distant and calm."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Mix it", body:"Blue + yellow.", answer:"<strong>Green</strong> (a secondary colour)."},
      {title:"Complement", body:"What is the complement of orange?", answer:"<strong>Blue</strong>."},
      {title:"Tint or shade?", body:"Red + white.", answer:"A <strong>tint</strong> (pink)."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Mixing complementaries and getting mud when you wanted a bright colour.","Thinking black and white are colours on the wheel.","Using every colour at full intensity — no focal point."],
    callout:{title:"Colour and culture", body:"Colours carry meaning in different cultures: in Zulu beadwork, colours and patterns can communicate messages, such as in beaded “love letters”."},
    activity:"Paint a 12-part colour wheel from only red, yellow and blue (plus white and black for a tint-and-shade strip).",
    activityAnswer:"A correct wheel shows 3 primaries, 3 secondaries between them and 6 tertiaries, with complementary pairs directly opposite.",
    examTip:"Use precise terms in analysis: “complementary contrast”, “warm palette”, “high-intensity colour”.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Complementary colours",d:"Colours opposite each other on the colour wheel"},{w:"Tint",d:"A colour mixed with white"},{w:"Analogous colours",d:"Colours next to each other on the wheel"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Red + yellow makes:",opts:["Green","Orange","Violet","Brown"],a:1,et:"A secondary colour."},
      {q:"The complement of red is:",opts:["Orange","Green","Blue","Yellow"],a:1,et:"Opposite on the wheel."},
      {q:"Adding black to a colour makes a:",opts:["Tint","Shade","Tone","Hue"],a:1,et:"Tint = white, tone = grey."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Colour is a language: know the wheel and you can create harmony, contrast and mood on purpose."},

  2: {emoji:"🧶", subtitle:"Grade 10 · How things feel — and the patterns they repeat",
    illusText:"Actual texture you can touch; implied texture you can only see.",
    hook:{title:"The Basotho blanket", body:"A Basotho blanket's woven wool feels thick and warm — that's <strong>actual texture</strong>. Its repeated motifs (like maize cobs or crowns) create <strong>pattern</strong>. Now look at a photograph of the blanket: you can't touch it, but you can still “feel” the texture through your eyes — that's <strong>implied texture</strong>."},
    intro:"<strong>Texture</strong> is how a surface feels or looks like it feels. <strong>Actual (tactile) texture</strong> can be touched: thick impasto paint, collage, woven fibres, carved wood. <strong>Implied (visual) texture</strong> is created with marks: hatching, stippling, dry brush, rubbing (<strong>frottage</strong>) and detailed rendering. Texture adds realism, interest and contrast. <strong>Pattern</strong> is a repeated element (a <strong>motif</strong>): <strong>regular</strong> (exact repetition — wallpaper, shweshwe fabric), <strong>irregular/random</strong> (natural — leopard spots), <strong>alternating</strong>, <strong>radial</strong> (from a centre) and <strong>progressive</strong> (changing gradually). Pattern creates rhythm and unity; breaking a pattern creates emphasis.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"✋ Actual texture", body:"Built up with materials: impasto, sand in paint, fabric, collage."},
      {t:"teal", title:"👁️ Implied texture", body:"Drawn or painted marks that suggest roughness, softness or shine."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔁 Pattern", body:"A repeated motif — regular, random, alternating, radial or progressive."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Actual or implied?", body:"A pencil drawing of tree bark.", answer:"<strong>Implied</strong> texture."},
      {title:"Pattern type", body:"Shweshwe fabric's small repeated design.", answer:"A <strong>regular</strong> pattern."},
      {title:"Technique", body:"Rubbing a crayon over paper on a rough surface.", answer:"<strong>Frottage</strong>."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Thinking texture only means rough.","Repeating a pattern so much the work becomes boring — vary it.","Drawing the same texture for every surface."],
    callout:{title:"Local textures", body:"Grass weaving, beadwork, corrugated iron and pressed-tin ceilings are rich South African sources of texture and pattern."},
    activity:"Make a texture sampler: six squares — three frottage rubbings from around school and three drawn textures (fur, metal, glass).",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. Rubbings show actual textures transferred to paper; drawn textures use hatching, stippling and highlights to imply surfaces.",
    examTip:"Describe how texture affects the viewer: “The thick impasto makes the sky feel restless.”",
    keyterms:[{w:"Implied texture",d:"Texture that is seen but can't be felt"},{w:"Motif",d:"An element repeated to form a pattern"},{w:"Frottage",d:"Making a rubbing of a textured surface"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Thick, raised paint creates:",opts:["Implied texture","Actual texture","Negative space","Perspective"],a:1,et:"Impasto can be touched."},
      {q:"A repeated design unit is a:",opts:["Motif","Hue","Contour","Plane"],a:0,et:"Motifs build patterns."},
      {q:"A pattern radiating from a centre is:",opts:["Random","Radial","Progressive","Linear"],a:1,et:"Like a sunflower or a rosette."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Texture invites touch, pattern creates rhythm — and breaking a pattern makes people look."},

  3: {emoji:"⚖️", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Arranging a composition so it feels right",
    illusText:"Symmetrical, asymmetrical, radial — three ways to balance visual weight.",
    hook:{title:"The perfect Instagram photo", body:"Photographers on Instagram often place the subject off-centre, using the “rule of thirds”, balance a big empty sky against a small person, or frame a perfectly symmetrical building. Without realising it, they're using the <strong>principles of design</strong> — balance and proportion — that painters have used for centuries."},
    intro:"<strong>Balance</strong> is how visual weight is distributed. <strong>Symmetrical (formal)</strong>: mirror image on both sides — calm, stable, formal (e.g. the Union Buildings' façade). <strong>Asymmetrical (informal)</strong>: different elements with equal visual weight — dynamic, natural. <strong>Radial</strong>: elements radiate from a centre. Visual weight increases with size, bright colour, contrast, detail and position. <strong>Proportion</strong> is the size relationship between parts (an adult figure is roughly 7–8 heads tall); <strong>scale</strong> is size compared with something else — artists exaggerate scale for emphasis (hierarchical scale makes important figures bigger). The <strong>rule of thirds</strong> divides the picture into a 3 × 3 grid; placing focal points on the lines or intersections creates interesting compositions.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🪞 Symmetry", body:"Formal and stable — but can feel static."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🎯 Asymmetry", body:"A small bright shape can balance a large dull one."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📏 Proportion", body:"About 7–8 heads tall for an adult; distortion is a deliberate choice."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Balance type", body:"A mandala or a sunflower head.", answer:"<strong>Radial</strong> balance."},
      {title:"Visual weight", body:"A small red circle on one side, a large grey square on the other.", answer:"<strong>Asymmetrical balance</strong> — bright colour adds weight to the small shape."},
      {title:"Proportion", body:"An adult figure drawn 4 heads tall looks…", answer:"<strong>Childlike or stylised</strong> — adults are about 7–8 heads tall."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Placing every subject dead centre.","Drawing heads too big for bodies by accident.","Confusing proportion (parts within a figure) with scale (size compared with other things)."],
    callout:{title:"Hierarchical scale", body:"In many ancient and religious artworks, the most important figure is the biggest — size shows status, not distance."},
    activity:"Take three phone photos of the same object: one symmetrical, one asymmetrical using the rule of thirds, and one radial. Compare the moods.",
    activityAnswer:"Usually: symmetrical feels formal and still; rule-of-thirds asymmetry feels natural and dynamic; radial draws the eye to the centre.",
    examTip:"In analysis, explain how balance affects mood, not just which type it is.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Asymmetrical balance",d:"Unequal elements with equal visual weight"},{w:"Proportion",d:"Size relationship between parts of a whole"},{w:"Rule of thirds",d:"Placing focal points on a 3 × 3 grid"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A mirror-image composition has:",opts:["Radial balance","Symmetrical balance","No balance","Asymmetrical balance"],a:1,et:"Formal balance."},
      {q:"An adult figure is roughly how many heads tall?",opts:["3–4","5","7–8","12"],a:2,et:"A useful drawing guide."},
      {q:"Which adds visual weight?",opts:["Dull colour","Bright colour","Blank space","Small size"],a:1,et:"Bright colours attract the eye."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Balance controls stability; proportion and scale control realism and emphasis."},

  4: {emoji:"🎯", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Leading the viewer's eye",
    illusText:"Contrast, isolation, convergence — three ways to create a focal point.",
    hook:{title:"The protest poster", body:"Anti-apartheid posters of the 1980s had seconds to catch a passer-by's eye: a raised fist in stark black on bright red, words in bold blocks, everything leading to one message. Designers used <strong>emphasis</strong> to create a focal point and <strong>rhythm</strong> to move the eye. The same tricks are used in today's billboards and album covers."},
    intro:"<strong>Emphasis</strong> makes one area the <strong>focal point</strong> — where the eye goes first. Create it with <strong>contrast</strong> (light/dark, colour, size, texture), <strong>isolation</strong> (a subject on its own), <strong>placement</strong> (centre or on the thirds), <strong>convergence</strong> (lines leading to it), and detail. <strong>Movement</strong> is the path the eye travels through the work, guided by lines, shapes and colours. <strong>Rhythm</strong> is created by repeating elements with variation: <strong>regular</strong> (even beat), <strong>flowing</strong> (curving, organic), <strong>progressive</strong> (changing step by step) and <strong>alternating</strong>. Artists also show actual movement with blurred lines, repeated figures or sequential images.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⭐ Focal point", body:"Contrast is the strongest tool: the brightest, sharpest or most different area wins."},
      {t:"teal", title:"➰ Movement", body:"Leading lines and repeated shapes guide the eye around the work."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🥁 Rhythm", body:"Repetition with variation, like a beat in music."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"How is emphasis created?", body:"One red umbrella in a crowd of black ones.", answer:"<strong>Colour contrast</strong> (and isolation)."},
      {title:"Rhythm type", body:"Waves painted with repeating curving lines.", answer:"<strong>Flowing</strong> rhythm."},
      {title:"Leading lines", body:"Railway tracks narrowing towards a figure.", answer:"<strong>Convergence</strong> — the lines lead to the focal point."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Having several equally strong focal points — the eye has nowhere to rest.","Repeating elements with no variation (boring rhythm).","Leading lines that point the eye out of the picture."],
    callout:{title:"The squint test", body:"Squint at your artwork: the area that stands out most is your focal point. Is it the one you intended?"},
    activity:"Design a protest or awareness poster for a current South African issue with one clear focal point and rhythm in the background.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. A strong poster has one dominant image or word, high contrast, a simple palette, and repeated background elements that lead to the focal point.",
    examTip:"Name the focal point and explain HOW it is emphasised.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Focal point",d:"The area that attracts attention first"},{w:"Rhythm",d:"Repetition of elements with variation"},{w:"Convergence",d:"Lines meeting at a point to direct the eye"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The strongest tool for creating a focal point is usually:",opts:["Contrast","Symmetry","Pattern","Texture"],a:0,et:"Difference draws the eye."},
      {q:"Lines leading to a subject create:",opts:["Convergence","Monochrome","Tint","Frottage"],a:0,et:"Leading lines."},
      {q:"Repetition with variation creates:",opts:["Rhythm","Balance only","Negative space","Shade"],a:0,et:"Like a musical beat."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Decide where the eye should go first, then lead it there."},

  5: {emoji:"🧩", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Making everything belong — without boring the viewer",
    illusText:"Unity holds it together; variety keeps it alive.",
    hook:{title:"The township mural", body:"A community mural in Soweto shows dozens of figures, shops, taxis and animals — yet it feels like one artwork. Every element shares the same bold outlines and colour palette (<strong>unity</strong>), while each figure has a different pose, size and story (<strong>variety</strong>). Great artworks balance both."},
    intro:"<strong>Unity</strong> (harmony) is the sense that all parts belong together. Create it with a limited <strong>colour palette</strong>, repeated shapes or lines, consistent style, proximity (grouping) and a clear theme. <strong>Variety</strong> adds interest through differences in size, shape, colour, texture and direction. Too much unity is boring; too much variety is chaotic. <strong>Contrast</strong> (strong difference) and <strong>harmony</strong> (similarity) work together. To <strong>analyse</strong> an artwork, use a structured method: <strong>describe</strong> (what do you see?), <strong>analyse</strong> (how are the elements and principles used?), <strong>interpret</strong> (what does it mean?) and <strong>judge</strong> (is it successful, and why?).",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🤝 Unity", body:"Shared colours, repeated shapes, consistent style."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🌈 Variety", body:"Differences that keep the viewer interested."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔍 Analysis method", body:"Describe → analyse → interpret → judge."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Unity tool", body:"A painting uses only blues and greens throughout.", answer:"A <strong>limited (analogous) palette</strong> creates unity."},
      {title:"Variety", body:"How could you add variety to a row of identical houses?", answer:"<strong>Change sizes, colours, angles or add figures</strong>."},
      {title:"Analysis step", body:"“The red figure in the centre is the focal point.” Which step?", answer:"<strong>Analyse</strong> (how elements and principles are used)."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Jumping to “I like it” without describing and analysing.","Too many unrelated colours and styles.","Copying a single element over and over without variation."],
    callout:{title:"Exam structure", body:"In visual analysis questions, work in order: describe, analyse, interpret, judge — and use art vocabulary."},
    activity:"Choose an artwork by a South African artist (in a gallery, book or online). Write a 4-paragraph analysis: describe, analyse, interpret, judge.",
    activityAnswer:"A strong answer describes subject and media; analyses at least three elements and two principles; interprets meaning with evidence and context; and judges success with reasons.",
    examTip:"Support every interpretation with visual evidence from the artwork.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Unity",d:"The sense that all parts belong together"},{w:"Variety",d:"Differences that create interest"},{w:"Interpretation",d:"Explaining the meaning of an artwork with evidence"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A limited colour palette mainly creates:",opts:["Variety","Unity","Chaos","Texture"],a:1,et:"Shared colours tie it together."},
      {q:"The first step of art analysis is to:",opts:["Judge","Describe","Interpret","Sell"],a:1,et:"Describe what you see."},
      {q:"Too much variety without unity looks:",opts:["Calm","Chaotic","Symmetrical","Monochrome"],a:1,et:"Balance them."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Unity ties a work together, variety keeps it alive — and a clear method helps you analyse any artwork."},
};

// ─── Module 1: Art History & Movements ──────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.arts.m1 = {
  0: {emoji:"🦏", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Thousands of years of art in southern Africa",
    illusText:"Blombos ochre · San rock art · the Mapungubwe golden rhino · Ndebele walls.",
    hook:{title:"The golden rhino", body:"In the 1930s, a small rhinoceros made of thin gold foil over a wooden core was found at Mapungubwe, near where South Africa, Botswana and Zimbabwe meet. Made about 800 years ago in a wealthy trading kingdom, it shows that sophisticated art was being made here long before colonisation. Southern Africa has one of the longest art histories on Earth."},
    intro:"<strong>African art traditions</strong> in southern Africa: <strong>Blombos Cave</strong> (Southern Cape) — engraved and drawn ochre more than 70 000 years old. <strong>San rock art</strong> — thousands of paintings and engravings, especially in the uKhahlamba-Drakensberg (a World Heritage Site); the <strong>eland</strong> appears often and is linked to spiritual beliefs and trance experiences. <strong>Mapungubwe</strong> (about 1220–1290) — a trading kingdom with gold objects including the golden rhino. <strong>Functional and ceremonial art</strong>: Zulu and Xhosa beadwork, Ndebele wall painting and beadwork, Venda and Tsonga wood carving, Zulu basketry and pottery, headrests and staffs. Many works combined beauty, function and meaning — status, identity, communication and spirituality.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🪨 Rock art", body:"San paintings show animals, people and spiritual experiences — not just “hunting scenes”."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🏆 Mapungubwe", body:"Gold, trade with the East African coast, and evidence of a complex society."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📿 Art in daily life", body:"Beadwork, pottery and wall painting carried identity and meaning."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Context", body:"Why does the eland appear so often in San rock art?", answer:"It was a <strong>powerful spiritual symbol</strong> linked to rain, ritual and trance."},
      {title:"Date", body:"About how old is the Mapungubwe golden rhino?", answer:"About <strong>800 years</strong> (13th century)."},
      {title:"Function", body:"Give one purpose of Zulu beadwork.", answer:"To show <strong>identity and status</strong> or communicate messages (e.g. courtship)."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Calling African art “primitive” — a colonial idea.","Thinking art must hang on a wall to be art.","Ignoring the meaning and function of traditional objects."],
    callout:{title:"Visit", body:"The Mapungubwe Collection is at the University of Pretoria Museums; San rock art can be seen at sites in the Drakensberg, the Cederberg and elsewhere."},
    activity:"Research one southern African art tradition. Describe its materials, techniques, function and meaning, and one place you can see it.",
    activityAnswer:"Example — Ndebele wall painting: pigments (now often acrylic) on plastered walls; bold geometric designs outlined in black; expresses identity and was traditionally painted by women; seen in Mpumalanga and at cultural villages.",
    examTip:"Always connect an artwork to its context: who made it, when, why and for whom.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Rock art",d:"Paintings and engravings on rock surfaces"},{w:"Ochre",d:"Natural earth pigment, red to yellow"},{w:"Functional art",d:"Art made to be used as well as admired"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The Mapungubwe golden rhino dates to about the:",opts:["1st century","13th century","18th century","20th century"],a:1,et:"About 800 years ago."},
      {q:"An animal often shown in San rock art:",opts:["Lion","Eland","Horse","Cow"],a:1,et:"A key spiritual symbol."},
      {q:"Blombos Cave is famous for ochre marks over:",opts:["700 years old","7 000 years old","70 000 years old","7 million years old"],a:2,et:"Some of the oldest known."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Southern Africa's art history is ancient, rich and meaningful — far older than colonial art."},

  1: {emoji:"🖼️", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Who painted Africa, and how they saw it",
    illusText:"Explorer paintings, “empty” landscapes and the first gallery purchase of a black artist's work.",
    hook:{title:"The empty landscape", body:"Many colonial-era landscape paintings show South Africa as vast, empty and waiting to be claimed — even when people lived there. These paintings weren't neutral; they helped tell a story that justified taking land. Learning to ask “Who is missing from this picture?” is a key art-history skill."},
    intro:"<strong>Colonialism</strong> shaped what art was made, collected and valued. <strong>Explorer and traveller artists</strong> (e.g. Thomas Baines, 19th century) recorded landscapes, wildlife and people, often from a European point of view. In the early 20th century, <strong>J.H. Pierneef</strong> painted stylised, often empty landscapes linked to Afrikaner identity, and <strong>Irma Stern</strong> painted expressive portraits of African people, admired but also debated for how they portray their sitters. African artists were largely excluded from art schools and galleries. <strong>Gerard Sekoto</strong>'s <em>Yellow Houses</em> (1940), showing Sophiatown life, became the first work by a black artist bought by the Johannesburg Art Gallery. Western museums also collected African objects, often without consent — leading to today's debates about <strong>restitution</strong>.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"👁️ The gaze", body:"Ask who made the image, for whom, and how the subjects are shown."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🏞️ Landscape and land", body:"Empty landscapes could support myths that the land was unoccupied."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🏛️ Restitution", body:"Debates about returning objects taken from Africa during colonialism."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Critical question", body:"A painting shows a beautiful valley with no people. What should you ask?", answer:"<strong>Who lived there, and why are they not shown?</strong>"},
      {title:"Milestone", body:"Why is Sekoto's <em>Yellow Houses</em> important?", answer:"It was the <strong>first work by a black artist bought by the Johannesburg Art Gallery</strong>."},
      {title:"Debate", body:"Why do some argue objects in European museums should return to Africa?", answer:"Many were <strong>taken without consent</strong> during colonial rule."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Treating colonial images as neutral records.","Ignoring artists excluded by the system.","Judging the past without explaining its context."],
    callout:{title:"Look again", body:"Galleries such as the Iziko South African National Gallery increasingly display colonial works with new labels that explain their context."},
    activity:"Compare a colonial-era landscape painting with a work by Gerard Sekoto. How does each show South Africa and its people?",
    activityAnswer:"A strong answer contrasts the empty, idealised land in many colonial landscapes with Sekoto's lived, peopled urban scenes, and links each to its artist's context.",
    examTip:"Use context: name the period, the artist's background and the social and political situation.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Colonial gaze",d:"Seeing and showing people and places from a coloniser's viewpoint"},{w:"Restitution",d:"Returning cultural objects to their communities"},{w:"Context",d:"The historical and social situation of an artwork"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Gerard Sekoto's Yellow Houses shows life in:",opts:["Sophiatown","Paris","Cape Town harbour","Mapungubwe"],a:0,et:"A vibrant Johannesburg suburb."},
      {q:"A key question for colonial landscapes is:",opts:["How expensive was it?","Who is missing?","What size is it?","Is it framed?"],a:1,et:"Who was left out of the story?"},
      {q:"Returning taken objects to their communities is:",opts:["Restoration","Restitution","Reproduction","Retouching"],a:1,et:"An ongoing debate."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Art is never neutral — ask who made it, who's shown, who's missing and why."},

  2: {emoji:"🌀", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · From Impressionism to Pop Art",
    illusText:"Impressionism → Expressionism → Cubism → Surrealism → Abstract Expressionism → Pop Art.",
    hook:{title:"Picasso's African connection", body:"In 1907, Pablo Picasso painted <em>Les Demoiselles d'Avignon</em>, with faces influenced by African and Iberian masks he had seen in Paris. It helped launch <strong>Cubism</strong>, one of the most important movements in modern art. Modern European art was deeply shaped by African art — a fact that was often left out of the story."},
    intro:"<strong>Modern art movements</strong> broke with realistic tradition. <strong>Impressionism</strong> (1870s, Monet) — quick brushstrokes capturing light and the moment. <strong>Post-Impressionism</strong> (Van Gogh, Cézanne) — expressive colour and structure. <strong>Expressionism</strong> (early 1900s) — distorted forms and intense colour to express emotion. <strong>Cubism</strong> (Picasso, Braque, from about 1907) — many viewpoints at once, fragmented geometric forms; influenced by African sculpture. <strong>Surrealism</strong> (1920s, Dalí, Magritte) — dreams and the unconscious. <strong>Abstract Expressionism</strong> (1940s–50s, Pollock) — gestural, non-representational. <strong>Pop Art</strong> (1950s–60s, Warhol) — mass media, advertising and celebrity. South African artists such as Irma Stern (Expressionism) engaged with these movements.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"☀️ Light and feeling", body:"Impressionists painted light; Expressionists painted emotion."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔷 Many viewpoints", body:"Cubism shows an object from several angles at once."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🥫 Popular culture", body:"Pop Art made art from ads, comics and celebrities."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Movement", body:"Melting clocks and dream-like scenes.", answer:"<strong>Surrealism</strong>."},
      {title:"Movement", body:"Fragmented faces seen from several angles.", answer:"<strong>Cubism</strong>."},
      {title:"Influence", body:"What influenced early Cubism?", answer:"<strong>African and Iberian sculpture</strong> (and Cézanne's structure)."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Mixing up Impressionism and Expressionism.","Telling the story of modern art without its African influences.","Memorising names without describing the visual characteristics."],
    callout:{title:"See the link", body:"Compare a Fang or Dan mask with an early Cubist face: the simplified, geometric planes are clearly related."},
    activity:"Make a timeline of six modern movements with dates, two artists, key visual characteristics and one example artwork each.",
    activityAnswer:"Check: correct order and approximate dates; characteristics described visually (brushwork, colour, form, subject); artists matched to the right movement.",
    examTip:"Learn two visual characteristics for each movement so you can identify unseen works.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Cubism",d:"Movement showing multiple viewpoints with geometric forms"},{w:"Expressionism",d:"Art that distorts form and colour to express emotion"},{w:"Pop Art",d:"Art using imagery from mass media and advertising"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Capturing light with quick brushstrokes is:",opts:["Cubism","Impressionism","Pop Art","Surrealism"],a:1,et:"Monet and friends."},
      {q:"Cubism was influenced by:",opts:["Comic books","African sculpture","Photography only","Medieval tapestries"],a:1,et:"Seen in Picasso's work around 1907."},
      {q:"Andy Warhol is linked to:",opts:["Surrealism","Pop Art","Impressionism","Cubism"],a:1,et:"Celebrity and soup cans."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Modern movements each broke a rule — and African art helped shape them."},

  3: {emoji:"✊🏾", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Art education, resistance and a changing nation",
    illusText:"Polly Street · Rorke's Drift · resistance posters · Butcher Boys.",
    hook:{title:"Art that fought apartheid", body:"In the 1980s, posters printed in community workshops called for freedom, boycotts and the release of Nelson Mandela. Jane Alexander's sculpture <em>Butcher Boys</em> (1985–86) — three seated, horned, mouthless figures — captured the brutality of the era. Under apartheid, art became a way to resist, record and imagine a different future."},
    intro:"Apartheid shut black artists out of formal art schools, so alternative spaces grew. <strong>Polly Street Art Centre</strong> (Johannesburg, 1950s, with Cecil Skotnes) trained many artists. The <strong>Rorke's Drift Art and Craft Centre</strong> (KwaZulu-Natal, from the 1960s) became famous for <strong>printmaking</strong> — linocuts and woodcuts by artists such as Azaria Mbatha and John Muafangejo telling biblical, historical and personal stories. <strong>Township art</strong> showed everyday life. In the 1960s Dumile Feni's powerful drawings expressed suffering. The 1980s brought <strong>resistance art</strong>: posters, T-shirts, murals and works such as Jane Alexander's <em>Butcher Boys</em>. After 1994, artists explored democracy, memory, identity and the Truth and Reconciliation Commission.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🏫 Alternative schools", body:"Polly Street and Rorke's Drift trained artists excluded by apartheid."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🖨️ Printmaking", body:"Cheap, reproducible — ideal for spreading images and messages."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📢 Resistance", body:"Art as protest: posters, murals and sculpture against apartheid."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Why printmaking?", body:"Why was printmaking popular for resistance art?", answer:"Prints are <strong>cheap and can be reproduced many times</strong> to spread a message."},
      {title:"Place", body:"Which centre is famous for linocuts by Azaria Mbatha?", answer:"<strong>Rorke's Drift</strong>."},
      {title:"Interpretation", body:"What might the mouthless figures of <em>Butcher Boys</em> suggest?", answer:"<strong>Silenced, dehumanised people</strong> — or the brutality and menace of the state."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Thinking SA art history starts in 1994.","Describing resistance art without its political context.","Forgetting the role of community workshops."],
    callout:{title:"See it", body:"Butcher Boys is in the Iziko South African National Gallery in Cape Town."},
    activity:"Design a linocut-style poster about a cause you care about, using bold black-and-white shapes and a short slogan.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. Strong designs use high contrast, simple shapes, a clear focal point and text that reads well — remember that a print reverses the image.",
    examTip:"Link each artwork to its political and social context, then analyse how its visual choices communicate the message.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Resistance art",d:"Art made to oppose oppression"},{w:"Linocut",d:"Relief print from a carved linoleum block"},{w:"Township art",d:"Art showing everyday life in townships"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Rorke's Drift became famous for:",opts:["Oil painting","Printmaking","Photography","Ceramics only"],a:1,et:"Linocuts and woodcuts."},
      {q:"Butcher Boys was made by:",opts:["William Kentridge","Jane Alexander","Irma Stern","Gerard Sekoto"],a:1,et:"1985–86."},
      {q:"Printmaking suited resistance art because it is:",opts:["Expensive","Reproducible","Slow","Secret"],a:1,et:"Many copies, quickly."},
    ],
    conclusion:"South African art resisted, recorded and reimagined — art history here is also political history."},

  4: {emoji:"🌟", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Artists shaping the conversation today",
    illusText:"Charcoal films, visual activism, Sophie in blue — SA artists on world stages.",
    hook:{title:"From Joburg to the world", body:"South African artists show in major museums and biennales around the world. William Kentridge makes films from charcoal drawings he erases and redraws; Zanele Muholi calls themself a visual activist and photographs Black LGBTQIA+ lives; Mary Sibande turns a domestic worker's uniform into a magnificent sculpture. Contemporary art asks questions about who we are now."},
    intro:"<strong>Contemporary art</strong> (roughly 1970s to today) uses any media — painting, photography, video, installation, performance, digital — and often deals with identity, history, power and society. South African examples: <strong>William Kentridge</strong> — animated films made by drawing in charcoal, filming, erasing and redrawing, exploring memory and Johannesburg's history; <strong>Zanele Muholi</strong> — photography and visual activism, including the self-portrait series <em>Somnyama Ngonyama</em> (“Hail the Dark Lioness”); <strong>Mary Sibande</strong> — sculptures and photographs of her alter ego “Sophie”, a domestic worker dressed in an extravagant Victorian-style uniform, exploring race, gender and labour; <strong>Nicholas Hlobo</strong> — works with rubber inner tubes and ribbon, often titled in isiXhosa, exploring masculinity and identity; <strong>Esther Mahlangu</strong> — Ndebele painting on global platforms.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🎞️ New media", body:"Video, installation, performance and photography are all art."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🪞 Identity", body:"Race, gender, sexuality, class and heritage are central themes."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🕰️ Memory", body:"Many artists revisit apartheid history to understand the present."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Match the artist", body:"Charcoal drawings erased and redrawn to make films.", answer:"<strong>William Kentridge</strong>."},
      {title:"Match the artist", body:"An alter ego called “Sophie” in a domestic worker's uniform.", answer:"<strong>Mary Sibande</strong>."},
      {title:"Interpretation", body:"Why might an artist use a domestic worker's uniform?", answer:"To explore <strong>race, gender, labour and dreams of freedom</strong> in South African history."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Thinking contemporary art must be painting or sculpture.","Describing an artwork without its themes.","Mixing up artists and their media."],
    callout:{title:"Go see art", body:"Zeitz MOCAA (Cape Town), the Johannesburg Art Gallery and university galleries regularly show contemporary South African art — many have free days."},
    activity:"Choose a contemporary South African artist. Write about their media, themes, one artwork and why it matters today.",
    activityAnswer:"A strong answer names the media, two themes, describes one work with visual detail, and explains its relevance to current South African issues.",
    examTip:"For unseen contemporary works, focus on media, process, themes and the viewer's experience.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Installation",d:"Artwork that transforms a space"},{w:"Visual activism",d:"Using images to fight for social change"},{w:"Alter ego",d:"An invented second identity"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"William Kentridge is known for:",opts:["Charcoal animation","Beadwork","Rock art","Pop Art"],a:0,et:"Draw, erase, redraw, film."},
      {q:"Zanele Muholi describes themself as a:",opts:["Visual activist","Architect","Potter","Fashion designer"],a:0,et:"Photography for change."},
      {q:"Mary Sibande's alter ego is called:",opts:["Sophie","Thandi","Nomsa","Grace"],a:0,et:"A domestic worker reimagined."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Contemporary SA artists use every medium to ask who we are — and who we could be."},

  5: {emoji:"🌍", subtitle:"Grade 12 · African art in a connected world",
    illusText:"Basotho blankets in Hollywood, Afrofuturism online, Zeitz MOCAA in a grain silo.",
    hook:{title:"Wakanda's wardrobe", body:"For the film <em>Black Panther</em> (2018), costume designer Ruth E. Carter drew on African designs including the Basotho blanket and the Zulu isicholo hat. Millions of people worldwide saw southern African design on screen. Global culture borrows from Africa constantly — raising questions about influence, credit and who benefits."},
    intro:"Art today moves across borders instantly. <strong>Global influence</strong>: international styles, digital art, street art and social media shape young African artists. <strong>Local influence</strong>: African forms, symbols and stories shape global fashion, film, music videos and design. <strong>Afrofuturism</strong> imagines African futures through technology and science fiction. Key questions: <strong>cultural appreciation</strong> (respectful borrowing with credit, permission and benefit to the source community) vs <strong>cultural appropriation</strong> (taking without understanding, credit or benefit); <strong>intellectual property</strong> for traditional designs; access to art. Institutions such as <strong>Zeitz MOCAA</strong> (opened 2017 in a converted grain silo in Cape Town) and art fairs bring African art to international audiences, while online platforms let artists sell directly.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔄 Two-way flow", body:"Africa influences the world; the world influences African artists."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⚖️ Appreciation vs appropriation", body:"Credit, permission, understanding and fair benefit make the difference."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🚀 Afrofuturism", body:"African culture plus technology and imagination."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Appreciation or appropriation?", body:"A global brand copies a Ndebele pattern for sneakers without credit or payment.", answer:"<strong>Appropriation</strong> — no credit, permission or benefit."},
      {title:"Institution", body:"Which museum opened in a Cape Town grain silo in 2017?", answer:"<strong>Zeitz MOCAA</strong>."},
      {title:"Global platform", body:"Give one way social media helps young artists.", answer:"They can <strong>build an audience and sell work directly</strong> without a gallery."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Treating all borrowing as theft — or all borrowing as fine.","Ignoring who benefits financially.","Forgetting that African artists also draw on global styles."],
    callout:{title:"Protecting heritage", body:"South African law has been updated to recognise indigenous knowledge, including traditional designs — a developing area of intellectual property."},
    activity:"Find an example of African design in global fashion, film or music. Decide whether it's appreciation or appropriation, using evidence.",
    activityAnswer:"A strong answer names the example, the source culture, whether credit/permission/payment were given, and reaches a reasoned judgement.",
    examTip:"In essay questions, argue both sides, then give your judgement with examples.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Cultural appropriation",d:"Taking elements of a culture without understanding, credit or benefit"},{w:"Afrofuturism",d:"Art imagining African futures through technology and fiction"},{w:"Globalisation",d:"The growing connection of cultures and economies worldwide"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Zeitz MOCAA is in:",opts:["Johannesburg","Cape Town","Durban","Pretoria"],a:1,et:"In a converted grain silo."},
      {q:"Borrowing culture with credit, permission and benefit is:",opts:["Appropriation","Appreciation","Plagiarism","Piracy"],a:1,et:"Respectful exchange."},
      {q:"Afrofuturism combines African culture with:",opts:["Technology and imagination","Only ancient history","Only landscapes","Only religion"],a:0,et:"Imagining futures."},
    ],
    conclusion:"African art shapes the world — make sure credit and benefit flow back to its sources."},
};

// ─── Module 2: Studio Practice ──────────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.arts.m2 = {
  0: {emoji:"✏️", subtitle:"Grade 10–12 · Seeing like an artist and drawing what you see",
    illusText:"Hatching · cross-hatching · stippling · blending — four ways to build tone.",
    hook:{title:"Draw what you see, not what you know", body:"Ask someone to draw an eye and they'll draw an almond with a circle. Look at a real eye and you'll see a wet highlight, eyelashes casting shadows and a lower lid with thickness. Drawing is mostly about <strong>looking</strong> — and anyone can learn it with practice."},
    intro:"Drawing skills: <strong>observation</strong> (draw what you see), <strong>contour</strong> drawing (outlines and edges; blind contour trains the eye), <strong>gesture</strong> drawing (quick lines capturing movement), <strong>tone</strong> (a range from white to black). Tonal techniques: <strong>hatching</strong> (parallel lines), <strong>cross-hatching</strong>, <strong>stippling</strong> (dots), <strong>blending</strong> and <strong>scribbling</strong>. Light on a form: highlight, mid-tone, core shadow, reflected light and <strong>cast shadow</strong>. <strong>Perspective</strong>: <strong>one-point</strong> (lines converge to one vanishing point on the horizon — a road), <strong>two-point</strong> (a building corner), <strong>atmospheric</strong> (distant objects paler and bluer). Media: graphite (H = hard/light, B = soft/dark), charcoal, ink, pastel. Measure proportions with a pencil held at arm's length.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"👁️ Observe", body:"Look at the subject more than the paper."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🌗 Tone", body:"A full tonal range from highlight to darkest shadow makes forms look 3D."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🛣️ Perspective", body:"Parallel lines converge to vanishing points on the horizon line."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Technique", body:"Building tone with tiny dots.", answer:"<strong>Stippling</strong>."},
      {title:"Pencil grade", body:"Which is darker: 2H or 4B?", answer:"<strong>4B</strong> (B pencils are softer and darker)."},
      {title:"Perspective", body:"A straight road disappearing into the distance.", answer:"<strong>One-point perspective</strong>."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Drawing symbols instead of observing.","Pressing too hard too early — build tone gradually.","Leaving out cast shadows, so objects float."],
    callout:{title:"Daily practice", body:"Ten minutes of drawing a day in a sketchbook improves your skills faster than one long session a week."},
    activity:"Draw a crumpled paper bag or a shoe from observation, using at least five tones and one tonal technique.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. Check for a full tonal range, accurate proportions, a cast shadow and consistent light direction.",
    examTip:"In practical work, show a full range of tone and a consistent light source.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Hatching",d:"Building tone with parallel lines"},{w:"Vanishing point",d:"The point where parallel lines appear to meet"},{w:"Cast shadow",d:"Shadow thrown by an object onto a surface"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Building tone with dots is:",opts:["Hatching","Stippling","Blending","Contour"],a:1,et:"Dots close together look darker."},
      {q:"Which pencil is softest and darkest?",opts:["2H","HB","6B","4H"],a:2,et:"Higher B = softer."},
      {q:"Two-point perspective is used for:",opts:["A road straight ahead","The corner of a building","A flat circle","Portraits only"],a:1,et:"Two vanishing points."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Look carefully, build tone gradually, and use perspective to create depth."},

  1: {emoji:"🖌️", subtitle:"Grade 10–12 · Two very different ways to paint",
    illusText:"Watercolour: light to dark, let the paper glow. Acrylic: dark or light in any order, dries fast.",
    hook:{title:"The sunset over the Drakensberg", body:"A watercolour sunset glows because light passes through thin washes and bounces off the white paper. An acrylic version can be bold, opaque and layered, with thick textured clouds. Same subject, completely different results — because each medium works differently."},
    intro:"<strong>Watercolour</strong> is transparent: work from <strong>light to dark</strong>, keep whites by leaving paper unpainted. Techniques: <strong>wet-on-wet</strong> (soft, blended edges), <strong>wet-on-dry</strong> (sharp edges), <strong>graded wash</strong>, <strong>glazing</strong> (thin layers over dry layers), dry brush, lifting with a tissue, salt texture. <strong>Acrylic</strong> is water-based but dries fast to a waterproof, flexible film; it can be thin (washes, glazes) or thick (<strong>impasto</strong>), and you can paint light over dark. Use a <strong>palette</strong> to mix, keep paint moist with a spray bottle, and wash brushes before acrylic dries. Process: sketch lightly → block in big areas → build mid-tones → add details and highlights. Colour mixing: mix from primaries plus white; avoid using black straight from the tube (it deadens colours).",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"💧 Watercolour", body:"Transparent: plan your whites and work light to dark."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🖌️ Acrylic", body:"Opaque or transparent, fast-drying, can layer light over dark."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧱 Big to small", body:"Block in large areas first, details last."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Technique", body:"Soft, blurry clouds blending into the sky.", answer:"<strong>Wet-on-wet</strong> watercolour."},
      {title:"Technique", body:"Thick, raised brushstrokes you can feel.", answer:"<strong>Impasto</strong> (acrylic or oil)."},
      {title:"Whites in watercolour", body:"How do you keep a bright white highlight?", answer:"<strong>Leave the paper unpainted</strong> (or use masking fluid)."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Overworking watercolour until it turns muddy.","Letting acrylic dry in brushes.","Starting with tiny details before blocking in."],
    callout:{title:"Look after brushes", body:"Rinse brushes immediately, reshape the tips and store them upright — good brushes last years."},
    activity:"Paint the same simple landscape twice: once in watercolour (wet-on-wet sky, wet-on-dry foreground) and once in acrylic (with impasto in one area). Compare.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. Watercolour should show transparency and preserved whites; acrylic should show opacity, layering and textured impasto.",
    examTip:"In your sourcebook, label technique experiments with the medium, method and what you learned.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Wet-on-wet",d:"Painting onto wet paper or paint for soft blends"},{w:"Glazing",d:"Thin transparent layers over dry paint"},{w:"Impasto",d:"Thick paint that keeps brush marks"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Watercolour is generally painted:",opts:["Dark to light","Light to dark","Only in black","Only thick"],a:1,et:"It's transparent."},
      {q:"Acrylic paint dries:",opts:["Very slowly","Quickly and waterproof","Never","Only in sunlight"],a:1,et:"Wash brushes quickly."},
      {q:"Sharp-edged watercolour shapes come from:",opts:["Wet-on-wet","Wet-on-dry","Salt","Lifting"],a:1,et:"Paint on dry paper."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Respect each medium's nature: watercolour's transparency, acrylic's speed and body."},

  2: {emoji:"🖨️", subtitle:"Grade 10–12 · Carving, inking and pulling prints",
    illusText:"Carve away what stays white; ink the raised surface; the print comes out reversed.",
    hook:{title:"A message in a hundred copies", body:"At Rorke's Drift in the 1960s and 70s, artists carved linoleum blocks and printed powerful black-and-white images that could be reproduced many times. In the 1980s, poster workshops printed resistance messages by the hundred. <strong>Printmaking</strong> is the art of making multiples — and it's still used for posters, T-shirts and fine art."},
    intro:"<strong>Printmaking</strong> transfers an image from a prepared surface (the <strong>matrix</strong>) to paper or fabric, producing an <strong>edition</strong> of prints. <strong>Relief</strong> printing: carve away areas that should stay white; ink sits on the raised surface (<strong>linocut</strong>, <strong>woodcut</strong>, stamps). <strong>Intaglio</strong>: ink sits in grooves cut or etched into a plate (etching, engraving, drypoint). <strong>Screen printing</strong> (serigraphy): ink is pushed through a mesh with a stencil (T-shirts, posters). <strong>Monoprint/monotype</strong>: a one-off print. The printed image is <strong>reversed</strong> — text must be carved backwards. Tools: lino cutters (V and U gouges), brayer (roller), bench hook, baren or press. Prints are signed and numbered in pencil (e.g. 3/20 = third print of an edition of 20). <strong>Safety</strong>: always cut away from your hand using a bench hook.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔪 Relief", body:"What you carve away stays white; what's left prints."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🪞 Reversal", body:"Prints are mirror images — reverse text before carving."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔢 Editions", body:"Numbered prints: 5/25 means the fifth print of 25."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Which process?", body:"Ink pushed through a mesh stencil onto a T-shirt.", answer:"<strong>Screen printing</strong>."},
      {title:"Reversal", body:"You carve “AMANDLA” normally on a lino block. What prints?", answer:"The word <strong>reversed (mirror image)</strong>."},
      {title:"Edition", body:"A print is marked 7/30.", answer:"It's the <strong>7th print in an edition of 30</strong>."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Forgetting to reverse text.","Cutting towards your hand.","Too much ink, filling in fine lines."],
    callout:{title:"Reduction prints", body:"In a reduction linocut, you carve and print the same block several times in different colours — the block is destroyed, so the edition is fixed."},
    activity:"Design and carve a simple linocut (10 × 10 cm) using a South African motif, and print an edition of three.",
    activityAnswer:"Check for strong contrast between carved and uncarved areas, reversed text if used, even inking, and prints signed and numbered (1/3, 2/3, 3/3).",
    examTip:"Know the difference between relief, intaglio and screen printing — where does the ink sit?",
    keyterms:[{w:"Relief print",d:"Print taken from the raised surface of a block"},{w:"Brayer",d:"Roller used to spread ink"},{w:"Edition",d:"A set of identical numbered prints"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"In a linocut, carved areas print as:",opts:["Black","White (unprinted)","Red","Grey"],a:1,et:"Ink sits on raised areas."},
      {q:"Text must be carved:",opts:["Upside down","Reversed","Larger","In pencil"],a:1,et:"Prints are mirror images."},
      {q:"Ink in grooves of a plate is:",opts:["Relief","Intaglio","Screen print","Collage"],a:1,et:"Etching and engraving."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Carve carefully, reverse your text and print a clean edition — printmaking multiplies your voice."},

  3: {emoji:"✂️", subtitle:"Grade 10–12 · Combining materials to say more",
    illusText:"Paper, fabric, found objects and paint — layered into one artwork.",
    hook:{title:"Art from the scrapyard", body:"South African artist Willie Bester built powerful artworks from scrap metal, found objects and paint to comment on apartheid and township life. A rusted tin can or an old ID document carries history that paint alone can't. <strong>Mixed media</strong> and <strong>collage</strong> let artists combine materials — and their meanings."},
    intro:"<strong>Collage</strong> is made by gluing papers, photographs, fabric and other flat materials onto a surface (from the French <em>coller</em>, to glue). <strong>Mixed media</strong> combines several media in one work — paint, drawing, collage, print, found objects. <strong>Assemblage</strong> is 3D collage of found objects. <strong>Photomontage</strong> combines cut photographs. Techniques: tearing vs cutting, layering, image transfer, gel medium, sanding back, stitching, and sealing with a varnish. Materials carry <strong>meaning</strong>: newspaper headlines, maps, packaging, fabric (like shweshwe) or personal items add context. Think about <strong>archival quality</strong> (acid-free glue and paper last longer) and unity — repeat colours or materials to tie the work together.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📰 Materials mean something", body:"A map, a receipt or a school uniform adds a story."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🧱 Layering", body:"Build up surfaces: glue, paint, sand back, add again."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔗 Unity", body:"Repeated colours, shapes or materials stop it looking messy."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Which term?", body:"A 3D artwork built from found objects.", answer:"<strong>Assemblage</strong>."},
      {title:"Meaning", body:"Why might an artist use old newspaper headlines?", answer:"To add <strong>historical or political context</strong> to the work."},
      {title:"Technique", body:"How can you make collage elements look like part of the painting?", answer:"<strong>Paint over and around them, glaze and sand back</strong> to integrate them."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Gluing random images with no concept.","Using too much glue (wrinkles).","Forgetting to seal the work."],
    callout:{title:"Reuse", body:"Collect interesting papers and objects in a box — packaging, tickets, magazines and fabric offcuts are free art materials."},
    activity:"Make a mixed-media self-portrait combining a drawn or painted face with collaged materials that represent your identity (maps, fabric, text).",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. A strong work combines media thoughtfully, uses materials with personal meaning and has unity through colour or repetition.",
    examTip:"Explain WHY you chose each material — the concept matters as much as the technique.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Collage",d:"Artwork made by gluing materials onto a surface"},{w:"Assemblage",d:"3D artwork from found objects"},{w:"Mixed media",d:"Combining different materials and techniques"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Collage comes from the French word for:",opts:["Paint","Glue","Cut","Colour"],a:1,et:"Coller = to glue."},
      {q:"A 3D work of found objects is:",opts:["Assemblage","Watercolour","Etching","Fresco"],a:0,et:"3D collage."},
      {q:"Willie Bester is known for using:",opts:["Only watercolour","Scrap and found materials","Only marble","Only video"],a:1,et:"Mixed media with history."},
    ],
    conclusion:"In mixed media, every material is a word — choose them for what they say."},

  4: {emoji:"🗿", subtitle:"Grade 10–12 · Working in three dimensions",
    illusText:"Add (model, build) or subtract (carve) — or cast from a mould.",
    hook:{title:"The wood that came alive", body:"Noria Mabasa, from Venda in Limpopo, has said that dreams led her to begin working in clay; she later carved huge, flowing figures from wood, finding forms already hinted at in the grain of the log. Sculpture is different from drawing: you can walk around it, touch it, and it changes with light and viewpoint."},
    intro:"<strong>Sculpture</strong> is 3D art. Methods: <strong>additive</strong> — building up material (modelling clay, constructing with cardboard, wire or wood, welding); <strong>subtractive</strong> — removing material (carving wood or stone); <strong>casting</strong> — pouring liquid material (plaster, resin, bronze) into a mould; and <strong>assemblage</strong>. Types: <strong>free-standing</strong> (in the round — seen from all sides) and <strong>relief</strong> (projects from a background — low or high relief). Consider <strong>form</strong>, <strong>mass</strong> and <strong>volume</strong>, <strong>space</strong> (positive and negative, open forms), <strong>texture</strong>, <strong>surface finish</strong>, <strong>scale</strong> and <strong>site</strong> (public sculpture). Clay: wedge to remove air, join with slip (score and slip), keep walls even, dry slowly, fire in a kiln. Armatures (wire skeletons) support modelled forms.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"➕ Additive", body:"Modelling and constructing — building up form."},
      {t:"teal", title:"➖ Subtractive", body:"Carving away from a block — you can't add back."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔄 In the round", body:"Design all sides: viewers walk around it."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Method", body:"Carving a figure from a log.", answer:"<strong>Subtractive</strong>."},
      {title:"Method", body:"A wire armature covered in papier-mâché.", answer:"<strong>Additive</strong> (constructing and modelling)."},
      {title:"Clay joint", body:"How do you join two pieces of clay?", answer:"<strong>Score and slip</strong> — scratch both surfaces, add slip, press together."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Designing only the front of a free-standing sculpture.","Trapping air in clay (it can explode in the kiln).","Thin, unsupported parts that break."],
    callout:{title:"Public art", body:"Look at public sculptures in your town: who do they honour, who chose them, and do they still represent the community?"},
    activity:"Make a small sculpture (max 20 cm) of an animal important in South African culture, using either clay or a wire armature with papier-mâché.",
    activityAnswer:"Check: interesting from all sides, stable base, appropriate method (additive), correct joining or support, and a considered surface finish.",
    examTip:"Use 3D vocabulary: mass, volume, in the round, relief, additive, subtractive.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Armature",d:"A supporting framework inside a sculpture"},{w:"Relief sculpture",d:"Sculpture projecting from a flat background"},{w:"Subtractive",d:"Removing material to create form"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Carving stone is:",opts:["Additive","Subtractive","Casting","Printing"],a:1,et:"Removing material."},
      {q:"Sculpture viewed from all sides is:",opts:["Relief","In the round","Flat","A collage"],a:1,et:"Free-standing."},
      {q:"To join clay pieces you should:",opts:["Use glue","Score and slip","Use water only","Nail them"],a:1,et:"Scratch, slip, press."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Think in 3D: build up, carve away or cast — and design every side."},

  5: {emoji:"📚", subtitle:"Grade 10–12 · Sourcebooks, portfolios and the NSC exam",
    illusText:"Sourcebook → concept → experiments → final work → reflection.",
    hook:{title:"The sourcebook that won the bursary", body:"When Lerato applied for a fine-arts degree, the selectors spent more time on her <strong>sourcebook</strong> than on her final paintings. They wanted to see how she thinks: her research, rough ideas, failed experiments and how she solved problems. A strong portfolio shows the journey, not just the destination."},
    intro:"In NSC Visual Arts, practical work is assessed through the <strong>sourcebook</strong> (visual journal) and the <strong>final artwork</strong>, plus the theory exam (visual literacy and art history). The <strong>sourcebook</strong> records the creative process: theme and concept development, research (artists, images, notes), <strong>visual research</strong> (photos, sketches from life), experiments with media and techniques, composition studies, reflection and evaluation. The process: choose a theme → brainstorm/mind map → research artists → collect primary sources (your own photos and drawings) → experiment → develop compositions → final work → reflect. A <strong>portfolio</strong> for university or bursary applications shows your best and most varied work with short written explanations. Avoid copying: use your own reference photos and show how artists influenced you.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📓 Sourcebook", body:"Show thinking: research, experiments, mistakes and decisions."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📸 Primary sources", body:"Your own photos and drawings, not just internet images."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🗂️ Portfolio", body:"Best and varied work, clearly presented with short explanations."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Primary source", body:"Is a photo you took of your grandmother's hands primary or secondary?", answer:"<strong>Primary</strong> — you created it."},
      {title:"Process", body:"What comes after artist research?", answer:"<strong>Experimenting</strong> with media and composition ideas."},
      {title:"Portfolio", body:"Should a portfolio include only finished works?", answer:"<strong>No</strong> — include sketches and process work that show thinking."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Filling the sourcebook only after the final artwork is done.","Copying an internet image as the final work.","No written reflection or annotations."],
    callout:{title:"Time management", body:"Plan backwards from the deadline: research, experiments and composition studies each need weeks, not days."},
    activity:"Choose a theme (e.g. “Home”, “Load shedding”, “Hair”). Create a sourcebook spread: a mind map, three primary-source photos or drawings, one artist reference and two media experiments.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. A strong spread shows a clear concept, original primary sources, a relevant artist with notes on what you learned, and annotated experiments.",
    examTip:"Annotate everything in your sourcebook: what you tried, why, and what you'll change.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Sourcebook",d:"Visual journal recording the creative process"},{w:"Concept",d:"The main idea behind an artwork"},{w:"Primary source",d:"Reference material you create yourself"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A sourcebook mainly shows:",opts:["Only the final artwork","The creative process","Marks","Prices"],a:1,et:"Research, experiments and reflection."},
      {q:"Your own reference photos are:",opts:["Primary sources","Secondary sources","Plagiarism","Not allowed"],a:0,et:"You made them."},
      {q:"Annotations should explain:",opts:["Nothing","What you tried and why","Only colours","Only names"],a:1,et:"Show your thinking."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Document your journey honestly — the process is part of the art, and examiners and selectors love it."},
};

